# Google Play Store Launch Guide — ICPay Mobile

This guide provides an end-to-end operational roadmap for publishing **ICPay Mobile** (`app.icpay.mobile`) to the **Google Play Store**. It covers account registration nuances, Google's strict 20-tester requirement, crypto/financial policy compliance, EAS cloud build pipelines, and production rollout.

---

## 1. Google Play Developer Account Setup & Requirements

To publish on Google Play, you need a Google Play Developer Account ($25 one-time registration fee).

### Personal Account vs. Organization Account (Critical Decision)

| Aspect | Personal Developer Account | Organization (Company) Account |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Registration Fee** | $25 USD (one-time) | $25 USD (one-time) |
| **Verification Requirements** | Government ID (Passport / National ID) + Proof of Address. | **D-U-N-S Number** (Dun & Bradstreet) + Official Company Registration Docs. |
| **Mandatory Closed Testing Rule** | **REQUIRED**: Must recruit **20 distinct testers** who remain opted into closed testing continuously for at least **14 consecutive days** before production access is unlocked. | **EXEMPT**: Organizations are **not** subjected to the mandatory 20-tester/14-day closed testing block and can apply directly for production. |
| **Public Developer Profile** | Shows personal legal name and physical address on the Play Store listing. | Shows corporate entity name and registered office address. |

> [!IMPORTANT]
> If registering as a **Personal Account**, Google strictly enforces the **20 testers for 14 days** rule. You cannot bypass this rule on personal accounts created after November 2023. If you have an established company or LLC, registering an **Organization Account** (with a D-U-N-S number) is significantly faster for going straight to production.

